# simulation artifacts
# A VCD IS AN ARTIFACT OF A RUN, NOT REPO STATE: it churns on every run, it is
# regenerated on demand, and nothing in this repository reads one back. So the
# rule is by EXTENSION and at any depth, and that is the whole point.
#
# THIS WAS PATCHED THREE TIMES BEFORE IT WAS STATED ONCE, and each patch is
# still visible in this file. pe_serdes.vcd and tb_pe_eth_mac.vcd were both
# named individually, because those are the two files that actually turned up.
# Then tb/tb_pe_pinmux.vcd got committed as a 40 KB binary blob on 2026-09-22,
# which is what produced the tb/*.vcd and sim/*.vcd patterns below -- two named
# DIRECTORIES, on the reasoning that a waveform is written "next to itself".
# That reasoning holds for tb/ and sim/ and for nowhere else: on 2026-09-23 a
# tb_pe_eth_mac.vcd turned up at the REPO ROOT and needed its own named rule
# (the second one, still in this file), and on 2026-09-26 a tb_pe_ctrl_r3.vcd
# turned up at the root again -- 4.4 MB, matched by nothing, and invisible in
# `git status` rather than merely uncommitted. A hardcoded name is not a rule;
# it is the previous occurrence, and it is why two of the three patches are
# still here.
#
# WHY A DIRECTORY-LISTED RULE IS THE WRONG SHAPE: it makes the ignore set a
# function of WHERE the tool happened to be run rather than of WHAT it wrote,
# so every new invocation directory is another incident, and the failure is
# silent -- an unignored artifact does not fail a gate, it just sits there.
# Measured before this rule was added: `git ls-files | grep .vcd` is EMPTY, so
# no waveform is repo state and a general rule orphans nothing. It also retires
# BOTH named rules above rather than adding a third beside them, and the
# negations further down (!...dist/, !diagrams/.part-*) are unaffected because
# a later rule wins only where it actually matches.
*.vcd

# The two directory-scoped rules are KEPT, not merely superseded: they document
# which directories the harnesses actually write to (regress/run_all.sh runs
# from sim/), and a rule that is only correct by accident ages badly when
# someone greps this file to answer "where do waveforms land?".

# Every TB writes its own <name>.vcd next to itself when run from tb/. The
# named rules above only covered two of them, so a freshly-added TB's waveform
# (tb/tb_pe_pinmux.vcd, 2026-09-22) got committed as a 40 KB binary blob. One
# pattern covers them all -- and a VCD is an artifact of a run, not repo state:
# it churns on every run, it is regenerated on demand, and nothing reads it.
tb/*.vcd

# regenerated by regress/run_all.sh (kept out of git: binary-ish, churns every run)
sim/*.vcd

# PlantUML sidecars are tracked beside their editable sources.
diagrams/.part-*

# Keep the checked-in dashboard bundle under dist/.
!tools/live-canvas/dashboard/dist/
!tools/live-canvas/dashboard/dist/**

# repowise local index/cache (a tool's database, not repo state)
.repowise/

# Generated simulation waveforms (regenerated by regress/run_all.sh).
# Superseded by the *.vcd rule above, which is why the name is gone and not
# merely still-listed: tb_pe_eth_mac.vcd was the second occurrence of a root-
# level waveform and the second hardcoded patch for the same class of debris.

# Manager/worker scratch state (the SDD task ledger; the durable record is WORKLOG.md)
.superpowers/sdd/
